      },
      "remediation": {
        "description": "A KMS key encrypts resources across multiple data classification domains. This means a single compromised key grants access to data at different sensitivity levels, collapsing the cryptographic isolation boundary.",
        "action": "Create dedicated KMS keys per sensitivity domain. Apply key policies that restrict usage to IAM roles operating within that domain. Rotate existing PHI/CDE data to new domain-exclusive keys. Use KMS key tags (sensitivity=phi) and SCPs to prevent cross-domain key usage at the organizational level.",
        "example": "{\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"cryptography\": {\n      \"kms_key_id\": \"arn:aws:kms:us-east-1:123:key/phi-exclusive\",\n      \"key_isolation\": {\n        \"is_exclusive_to_domain\": true,\n        \"domain_count\": 1,\n        \"mixed_classification\": false\n      }\n    },\n    \"tags\": {\n      \"data-classification\": \"phi\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n"
